">FTC Staff Opinion and Interpretation of the FDCPA</span></span></div>=0A<=
br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-align: left; fo=
nt-size: 19px;"> Bruce, <br><br>If you=E2=80=99ve ever taken the time to si=
t and review consumer law, such as the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, =
in an attempt to understand and obtain concrete answers to what=E2=80=99s f=
air, what=E2=80=99s appropriate, what=E2=80=99s sufficient and what=
=E2=80=99s legal, you may have walked away with a vague understanding and w=
ay more questions than you initially had. When I started out in credit repa=
ir, in my quest to gain a thorough understanding of the laws we rely on to =
get results for our clients, one of my go to resources was the Federal Trad=
e Commission's staff opinion and interpretation of these laws. After all, t=
hey are the governing body responsible for enforcing regulation against unf=
air and deceptive practices. <br><br>As we begin to wrap up our lessons on =
Debt Collection and understanding the Validation and Verification process, =
today we will take a look at some of=0Athe interpretations of the law by th=
e FTC concerning the <span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; fon=
t-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: underline;" class=
=3D"">circumstances under which a debt collector may report a "charged-off =
debt" to a consumer reporting agency under the Fair Debt Collection Practic=
es Act</span>. Hopefully, the answers to the questions below will give you =
a better understanding of the FDCPA and how Debt Collectors and Debt Buyers=
 are expected to respond and act in accordance to the law. <br><b style=3D"=
margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>Q:=
</b><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit=
; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit; font-style: italic;" clas=
s=3D""> "Is it permissible under the FDCPA for a debt collector to report c=
harged-off debts to a consumer reporting agency during the term of the 30-d=
ay validation period detailed in Section <span=0Astyle=3D"color: #0b3861; f=
ont-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration=
: inherit;" class=3D"">1692g</span>?" <br></span><b style=3D"margin: 0; out=
line: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>FTC Staff Opinio=
n:</b> Yes. As stated in the Commission's Staff Commentary on the FDCPA (co=
py enclosed), a debt collector may accurately report a debt to a consumer r=
eporting agency within the thirty day validation period (p. 50103). We do n=
ot regard the action of reporting a debt to a consumer reporting agency as =
inconsistent with the consumer's dispute or verification rights under <span=
 style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; line-heigh=
t: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">=C2=A7 1692g</span>. II. =
<br><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight:=
 normal;"><br>Q:</b> <span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; fon=
t-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;=0Afont-s=
tyle: italic;" class=3D"">"Is it permissible under the FDCPA for a debt col=
lector to report, or continue to report, a consumer's charged-off debt to a=
 consumer reporting agency after the debt collector has received, but not r=
esponded to, a consumer's written dispute during the 30-day validation peri=
od detailed in <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-weig=
ht: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">=
=C2=A7 1692g</span>?" <br></span><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padd=
ing: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>FTC Staff Opinion:</b> As you kn=
ow, Section <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-weight:=
 bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">1692g(b)=
</span> requires the debt collector to cease collection of the debt at issu=
e if a written dispute is received within the 30-day validation period unti=
l verification is obtained. Because we believe that reporting a charged-off=
 debt to a consumer=0Areporting agency, particularly at this stage of the c=
ollection process, constitutes "collection activity" on the part of the col=
lector, our answer to your question is No. Although the FDCPA is unclear on=
 this point, we believe the reality is that debt collectors use the reporti=
ng mechanism as a tool to persuade consumers to pay, just like dunning lett=
ers and telephone calls. Of course, if a dispute is received after a debt h=
as been reported to a consumer reporting agency, the debt collector is obli=
gated by Section <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-we=
ight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">169=
2e(8)</span> to inform the consumer reporting agency of the dispute. III. <=
br><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: =
normal;"><br>Q:</b> <span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; font=
-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit; font-styl=
e: italic;" class=3D"">"Is it=0Apermissible under the FDCPA to cease collec=
tion of a debt rather than respond to a written dispute from a consumer rec=
eived during the 30-day validation period?" </span><br><b style=3D"margin: =
0; outline: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>FTC Staff =
Opinion:</b> Yes. There is nothing in the FDCPA that requires a debt collec=
tor to continue collecting a debt after a written dispute is received. Furt=
her, there is nothing in the FDCPA that requires a response to a written di=
spute if the debt collector chooses to abandon its collection effort with r=
espect to the debt at issue. (Smith v. Transworld Systems, Inc., 953 F.2d 1=
025, 1032 (6th Cir. 1992). IV. <br><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; pa=
dding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>Q:</b> <span style=3D"color: i=
nherit; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; tex=
t-decoration: inherit; font-style: italic;" class=3D"">"Would the following=
 action by a debt collector constitute=0Acontinued collection activity unde=
r <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; lin=
e-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">=C2=A7 1692g(b)</s=
pan>: reporting a charged-off consumer debt to a consumer reporting agency =
as disputed in accordance with <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: in=
herit; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" =
class=3D"">=C2=A7 1692e(8)</span>, when the debt collector became aware of =
the dispute when the consumer sent a written dispute to the debt collector =
during the 30-day validation period, and no verification of the debt has be=
en provided by the debt collector?"<br></span><b style=3D"margin: 0; outlin=
e: none; padding: 0; mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"><br>FTC Staff Opinion:<=
/b> " Yes. As stated in our answer to Question II, we view reporting to a c=
onsumer reporting agency as a collection activity prohibited by =C2=A7 <spa=
n style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold;=0Aline-he=
ight: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">1692g(b)</span> after =
a written dispute is received and no verification has been provided. Again,=
 however, a debt collector must report a dispute received after a debt has =
been reported under <span style=3D"color: #0b3861; font-size: inherit; font=
-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">=
=C2=A7 1692e(8)</span>. <br><br> Bruce, if you=E2=80=99ve ever come across =
any of these actions in dealing with a collection agency during the dispute=
 process and were unsure of how to respond due to the ambiguity of the law,=
 hopefully today=E2=80=99s content of FTC Staff Opinion regarding the FDCPA=
 gave some insight and clarity to how you can deal with these circumstances=
 moving forward. <br><br>Resource: <a href=3D"https://educreditsolutions.ac=
